1
00:00:23,565 --> 00:00:25,942
Hey, what-- What is going on here, please?

2
00:00:25,943 --> 00:00:27,193
I need to scan you.

3
00:00:27,194 --> 00:00:28,946
Please, sir. Be still, sir.

4
00:00:49,049 --> 00:00:51,051
You can come in. Still clear.

5
00:00:51,927 --> 00:00:53,303
Clear of what?

6
00:00:54,888 --> 00:00:55,931
I'm sorry--

7
00:01:03,772 --> 00:01:06,566
I'm Colonel Jay Stringer,
Great Lakes National Guard.

8
00:01:06,567 --> 00:01:09,611
This is Dr. Janet Sharp from the CDC.
Can you have a seat?

9
00:01:12,239 --> 00:01:13,240
Okay.

10
00:01:14,032 --> 00:01:17,076
Three weeks ago,
the first case of Hema Aetho 26

11
00:01:17,077 --> 00:01:18,661
was confirmed in Chicago.

12
00:01:18,662 --> 00:01:22,498
It spread quickly, one of
the highest fatality rates ever recorded.

13
00:01:22,499 --> 00:01:26,461
Four days ago,
we thought we identified our patient zero.

14
00:01:27,713 --> 00:01:29,548
Her name was Camilla Holder.

15
00:01:30,382 --> 00:01:34,719
What do you mean "was"? What did you say?

16
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:36,930
By the time we found her,
she had been dead for a while.

17
00:01:37,556 --> 00:01:40,474
Well before any
previously-documented deaths.

18
00:01:40,475 --> 00:01:43,312
I'm sorry to have to do this but…

19
00:01:44,021 --> 00:01:46,815
we need you to confirm
that this is your sister.

20
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:11,256
That's her.

21
00:02:14,635 --> 00:02:16,803
They're calling it the Black Iris Plague.

22
00:02:18,555 --> 00:02:21,141
You said
you thought you found patient zero.

23
00:02:39,201 --> 00:02:41,245
- What?
- I'm sorry. It was my fault.

24
00:02:43,997 --> 00:02:45,581
It's all my fault.

25
00:02:45,582 --> 00:02:46,667
I'm sorry.

26
00:02:47,835 --> 00:02:52,089
The reason they wanted you
to visually identify Camilla

27
00:02:52,673 --> 00:02:56,844
is because you and him

28
00:02:57,970 --> 00:03:01,014
both appear to be Dr. Ryan Holder,

29
00:03:01,557 --> 00:03:03,432
who, according to our records,

30
00:03:03,433 --> 00:03:06,060
died ten years ago
in a hyper loop accident.

31
00:03:06,061 --> 00:03:09,772
This Ryan Holder, despite dying later,

32
00:03:09,773 --> 00:03:13,360
was the first infected, our patient zero.

33
00:03:13,902 --> 00:03:15,945
We traced his movements.

34
00:03:15,946 --> 00:03:19,324
He walked out of the Shikaakwa Park cube
four weeks ago.

35
00:03:21,952 --> 00:03:23,161
With you.

36
00:03:24,246 --> 00:03:27,249
Just like the two of you did yesterday.

37
00:03:29,168 --> 00:03:30,419
But you're not sick.

38
00:03:32,588 --> 00:03:35,299
How can you be dead and here right now?

39
00:03:36,216 --> 00:03:38,969
We understand you're in shock.

40
00:03:40,846 --> 00:03:44,892
But the city is losing
20,000 people a day.

41
00:03:48,187 --> 00:03:53,025
There's gonna be further testing
and many more questions to come.

42
00:04:01,658 --> 00:04:05,996
Jason 2 said that
my Jason made decisions in the Corridor

43
00:04:06,663 --> 00:04:09,208
that created other versions of themselves.

44
00:04:10,501 --> 00:04:11,627
Yeah.

45
00:04:12,669 --> 00:04:19,259
Our other versions picked up
this virus and brought it back…

46
00:04:21,303 --> 00:04:22,638
before we got here.

47
00:04:29,561 --> 00:04:32,773
I'm responsible for the deaths
of tens of thousands of humans.

48
00:04:37,611 --> 00:04:39,112
We both are.

49
00:06:10,537 --> 00:06:15,792
So, in your life,
how long have I been gone?

50
00:06:17,085 --> 00:06:18,670
Fifteen years.

51
00:06:22,508 --> 00:06:26,845
Is your mother alive in that life?

52
00:06:28,805 --> 00:06:29,806
No.

53
00:06:31,767 --> 00:06:33,852
My Anne died four years ago.

54
00:06:34,895 --> 00:06:35,896
I'm sorry.

55
00:06:38,857 --> 00:06:39,858
I'm better now.

56
00:06:40,901 --> 00:06:42,653
I still miss her every day.

57
00:06:45,906 --> 00:06:47,991
How did you know to come here?

58
00:06:48,659 --> 00:06:50,284
I didn't know.

59
00:06:50,285 --> 00:06:54,915
I saw the bakery and it looked the same.

60
00:06:55,916 --> 00:07:00,420
I think I just hoped

61
00:07:00,963 --> 00:07:03,215
this place might be the same too.

62
00:07:04,299 --> 00:07:06,343
But I didn't mean for you to see me.

63
00:07:09,763 --> 00:07:11,223
Maybe that's not honest.

64
00:07:13,392 --> 00:07:15,060
Maybe I was being selfish.

65
00:07:18,230 --> 00:07:19,231
Either way…

66
00:07:21,275 --> 00:07:25,028
I know this is complicated.

67
00:07:25,946 --> 00:07:28,782
I'm really sorry.

68
00:07:29,950 --> 00:07:33,871
Well, it's an experience
I didn't see coming.

69
00:07:36,623 --> 00:07:41,962
But I'm sure it's less complicated
for me than it is for you.

70
00:07:45,132 --> 00:07:49,761
Right now, it's kind of tearing us apart.

71
00:07:52,055 --> 00:07:56,268
And Charlie, he lost his whole world.

72
00:07:58,187 --> 00:07:59,270
He's really missing it.

73
00:07:59,271 --> 00:08:02,441
And for Jason, it's the box itself

74
00:08:03,817 --> 00:08:05,277
become like a

75
00:08:07,154 --> 00:08:08,322
compulsion.

76
00:08:09,698 --> 00:08:11,783
He just needs to understand it.

77
00:08:14,453 --> 00:08:16,371
No matter how much damage it does.

78
00:08:17,706 --> 00:08:18,957
And for you?

79
00:08:21,627 --> 00:08:23,712
You could see it before I could.

80
00:08:26,465 --> 00:08:28,717
It's brought me back to someplace…

81
00:08:31,053 --> 00:08:32,136
old.

82
00:08:35,182 --> 00:08:36,183
As a kid,

83
00:08:37,808 --> 00:08:39,269
I never knew

84
00:08:40,687 --> 00:08:46,400
which was the real version of my father.

85
00:08:46,401 --> 00:08:49,988
The one who was kind

86
00:08:51,281 --> 00:08:52,658
or the one who…

87
00:08:55,452 --> 00:08:57,120
hurt me, you know?

88
00:08:58,664 --> 00:09:04,085
Then I was faced with
all these versions of my husband

89
00:09:04,086 --> 00:09:08,590
and it just went right back to when I was…

90
00:09:21,562 --> 00:09:22,688
<i>Mija</i>.

91
00:09:23,438 --> 00:09:25,148
Oh, Papa.

92
00:09:58,265 --> 00:09:59,433
Okay.

93
00:10:02,186 --> 00:10:03,562
Please.

94
00:10:04,646 --> 00:10:06,356
I just need some help.

95
00:10:18,410 --> 00:10:20,078
I just need some help.

96
00:11:22,850 --> 00:11:24,309
Oh, my God!

97
00:11:31,733 --> 00:11:33,318
I call it the Pyramid.

98
00:11:34,820 --> 00:11:35,821
Why a pyramid?

99
00:11:36,655 --> 00:11:38,489
Well, the Mayan and Egyptian pyramids

100
00:11:38,490 --> 00:11:40,450
were literally built
to be portals to the afterlife.

101
00:11:41,451 --> 00:11:42,869
Other worlds.

102
00:11:42,870 --> 00:11:43,996
Seemed appropriate.

103
00:11:47,541 --> 00:11:49,417
- And the shape was more efficient.
- There you go.

104
00:11:49,418 --> 00:11:51,712
Saved some surface area
for the adaptive shielding.

105
00:11:54,464 --> 00:11:55,924
Is that you?

106
00:11:58,427 --> 00:11:59,720
My partner in this project.

107
00:12:00,846 --> 00:12:01,847
You are ridiculous.

108
00:12:07,060 --> 00:12:09,021
And you must be Blair.

109
00:12:10,189 --> 00:12:11,522
First one in, right?

110
00:12:11,523 --> 00:12:12,524
Yeah.

111
00:12:13,192 --> 00:12:15,359
Yeah, I was the idiot
who volunteered for that.

112
00:12:15,360 --> 00:12:16,652
Hello.

113
00:12:16,653 --> 00:12:18,655
I would love to hear
about your experience sometime.

114
00:12:20,616 --> 00:12:21,867
And what's this?

115
00:12:22,784 --> 00:12:25,453
- It's a worlds chart.
- You're mapping them?

116
00:12:25,454 --> 00:12:27,288
It's mostly data entry at this point,

117
00:12:27,289 --> 00:12:30,501
but eventually we hope to organize it
in more meaningful ways.

118
00:12:31,084 --> 00:12:32,543
How many have you mapped?

119
00:12:32,544 --> 00:12:34,213
We've visited 74 worlds.

120
00:12:36,048 --> 00:12:38,217
This is HP-24.

121
00:12:40,469 --> 00:12:42,094
We have the basics at a glance.

122
00:12:42,095 --> 00:12:46,182
Who visited, when,
technological assets, which…

123
00:12:46,183 --> 00:12:48,185
is of primary interest to us.

124
00:12:48,727 --> 00:12:51,688
HP-24 has
the most incredible smart cities.

125
00:12:53,148 --> 00:12:55,526
- You joining us for dinner?
- No.

126
00:12:56,109 --> 00:12:58,194
We have a training session
with the new scout team.

127
00:12:58,195 --> 00:13:00,697
Well, I guess we'll catch up later.

128
00:13:04,660 --> 00:13:05,743
Scouts?

129
00:13:05,744 --> 00:13:08,329
Well, we used to send teams out
on missions constantly,

130
00:13:08,330 --> 00:13:11,374
trying to find useful worlds,
setting up trades.

131
00:13:11,375 --> 00:13:13,627
About a year ago,
we stopped using the box.

132
00:13:14,378 --> 00:13:16,213
This bad boy needed all our attention.

133
00:13:16,922 --> 00:13:18,257
Is that Ryan Holder?

134
00:13:19,800 --> 00:13:21,385
Not the one you know.

135
00:13:22,094 --> 00:13:23,553
I recruited him from this world.

136
00:13:23,554 --> 00:13:24,555
Ryan.

137
00:13:28,225 --> 00:13:29,851
I want you to meet a friend of mine.

138
00:13:29,852 --> 00:13:31,060
- This is Blair.
- Hi.

139
00:13:31,061 --> 00:13:32,312
Hi.

140
00:13:32,896 --> 00:13:34,856
Do you know another version of me?

141
00:13:34,857 --> 00:13:36,232
Just in passing.

142
00:13:36,233 --> 00:13:40,069
Ryan is developing a patch that does
a 30-day, controlled release of the drug.

143
00:13:40,070 --> 00:13:41,863
- Yeah.
- It lasts a month?

144
00:13:41,864 --> 00:13:44,156
You actually don't
even need to shoot up anymore.

145
00:13:44,157 --> 00:13:46,033
You just slap it on your arm,
you're good to go.

146
00:13:46,034 --> 00:13:47,118
We are so close.

147
00:13:47,119 --> 00:13:48,661
We've been running trials, and we're at…

148
00:13:48,662 --> 00:13:50,329
- What? Two weeks already?
- Two weeks.

149
00:13:50,330 --> 00:13:51,664
Two weeks already, yeah.

150
00:13:51,665 --> 00:13:53,584
- I'll let you get back to it.
- Thank you, sir.

151
00:13:56,712 --> 00:13:58,921
How did you build it in this world?

152
00:13:58,922 --> 00:14:01,424
Well, we sourced the trickier components
from other worlds.

153
00:14:01,425 --> 00:14:03,259
Ran the rest
through Velocity Tech's R & D.

154
00:14:03,260 --> 00:14:04,344
Is it functional?

155
00:14:06,680 --> 00:14:07,723
Ish.

156
00:14:08,682 --> 00:14:09,849
Ish?

157
00:14:09,850 --> 00:14:11,602
Power is the main issue.

158
00:14:12,394 --> 00:14:15,229
It's so big,
it can only run for 20 minutes at a time.

159
00:14:15,230 --> 00:14:17,149
Someone could get stuck in another world.

160
00:14:17,733 --> 00:14:19,526
Okay, so then how are you powering it?

161
00:14:20,319 --> 00:14:21,903
You don't wanna know.

162
00:14:21,904 --> 00:14:25,115
I don't, but obviously
you have to tell me. So just…

163
00:14:27,451 --> 00:14:28,784
Okay.

164
00:14:28,785 --> 00:14:31,704
- It's just a skosh nuclear.
- Just a little bit?

165
00:14:31,705 --> 00:14:33,080
The adaptive shielding load is--

166
00:14:33,081 --> 00:14:35,209
Ninety-X compared to the OG Box.

167
00:14:36,335 --> 00:14:37,502
- Yeah.
- And so you were trying

168
00:14:37,503 --> 00:14:39,421
to solve your power issues with fission?

169
00:14:41,798 --> 00:14:43,549
- What? No good?
- Jesus Christ, Leight.

170
00:14:43,550 --> 00:14:45,886
What? What?

171
00:14:48,889 --> 00:14:50,307
Why did you make it so big?

172
00:14:51,183 --> 00:14:54,435
Okay, listen, I know it's been a while
but you do remember the original dream.

173
00:14:54,436 --> 00:14:55,479
Right?

174
00:14:56,396 --> 00:14:59,858
Through this door are worlds that have
cured diseases that still ravage ours.

175
00:15:00,359 --> 00:15:03,069
Through this door are worlds
that have reversed climate change,

176
00:15:03,070 --> 00:15:05,738
saved species from extinction,
crushed poverty.

177
00:15:05,739 --> 00:15:08,116
Through this door are worlds
that have ended war.

178
00:15:09,409 --> 00:15:10,953
And we will visit these worlds.

179
00:15:12,162 --> 00:15:14,580
We will find the best technology,
the brightest ideas

180
00:15:14,581 --> 00:15:16,083
and we will bring them home.

181
00:15:17,042 --> 00:15:18,043
Yeah.

182
00:15:18,836 --> 00:15:21,880
I had a lot of time to think about
your vision when I was trapped inside it.

183
00:15:25,926 --> 00:15:26,927
Yeah.

184
00:15:30,681 --> 00:15:32,015
Wanna look inside?

185
00:15:34,434 --> 00:15:36,811
- Of course I do.
- Yeah.

186
00:15:36,812 --> 00:15:38,938
I have been thinking
about the cube in the pool.

187
00:15:38,939 --> 00:15:40,356
Personal life going that well, huh?

188
00:15:40,357 --> 00:15:43,150
No, shut up. Can I just see those photos
that you showed me last time?

189
00:15:43,151 --> 00:15:44,402
- Why?
- You know,

190
00:15:44,403 --> 00:15:47,905
Mom asked if I could bring her by
your place to see the kids this weekend.

191
00:15:47,906 --> 00:15:50,200
You haven't told her you
and Kendra separated?

192
00:15:56,748 --> 00:15:58,625
Dessen/Stewart.

193
00:16:00,961 --> 00:16:02,171
Choke on it.

194
00:16:10,304 --> 00:16:12,221
Motherfucker.

195
00:16:12,222 --> 00:16:13,473
What?

196
00:16:14,349 --> 00:16:16,518
That is the Velocity Tech logo.

197
00:16:19,104 --> 00:16:20,521
Leighton Vance's company.

198
00:16:20,522 --> 00:16:21,814
Yeah. Cool.

199
00:16:21,815 --> 00:16:23,734
Wires and metal and st…

200
00:16:24,610 --> 00:16:26,360
What the shit, Sheila?

201
00:16:26,361 --> 00:16:27,904
Skip to the end.

202
00:16:27,905 --> 00:16:31,866
So what is highly-advanced,
not-yet-available in stores,

203
00:16:31,867 --> 00:16:34,702
Velocity-labeled technology doing

204
00:16:34,703 --> 00:16:37,706
in some weird-ass cube
in a fucking abandoned school?

205
00:16:40,292 --> 00:16:42,878
These people are in on
whatever Velocity is doing.

206
00:16:44,296 --> 00:16:45,547
You figured it out.

207
00:16:47,633 --> 00:16:53,304
These people are clearly
super sophisticated corporate spies.

208
00:16:53,305 --> 00:16:55,473
Look, I know you don't give a shit
about the family business,

209
00:16:55,474 --> 00:16:58,893
but Velocity is destroying MacNamara Tech,

210
00:16:58,894 --> 00:17:03,147
and I need to stop them
and these people are the link.

211
00:17:03,148 --> 00:17:08,569
So, pretty please
with a go fuck yourself on top, help me.

212
00:17:21,708 --> 00:17:24,086
Seriously, why build to this scale?

213
00:17:25,796 --> 00:17:30,843
I went to this world, XC-89, that had been
polluted within an inch of its life.

214
00:17:32,094 --> 00:17:33,970
Carbon levels spiked to 700 ppm.

215
00:17:33,971 --> 00:17:35,180
- Jesus.
- Yeah.

216
00:17:36,014 --> 00:17:38,808
Their scientists were able
to build carbon scrubbers

217
00:17:38,809 --> 00:17:42,770
that could filter 80 metric tons
of the atmosphere a day.

218
00:17:42,771 --> 00:17:45,189
Pulled their planet
right back from the brink.

219
00:17:45,190 --> 00:17:47,860
I brought those plans
to many hurting worlds.

220
00:17:48,569 --> 00:17:51,488
But it'll take years, in some cases,
to build them.

221
00:18:04,918 --> 00:18:05,961
My God.

222
00:18:07,462 --> 00:18:08,463
Yeah.

223
00:18:09,798 --> 00:18:12,008
You could drive a semitruck through here.

224
00:18:12,009 --> 00:18:13,010
Exactly.

225
00:18:13,677 --> 00:18:15,304
Or a small army.

226
00:18:16,471 --> 00:18:18,515
Or refugees from a dying planet.

227
00:18:19,558 --> 00:18:22,019
Yeah, of course.

228
00:18:24,646 --> 00:18:26,607
And has anyone traveled in this Corridor?

229
00:18:27,649 --> 00:18:29,985
No. No one has.

230
00:18:30,527 --> 00:18:31,695
It's too dangerous.

231
00:18:32,529 --> 00:18:34,156
Especially with the power issue.

232
00:18:35,699 --> 00:18:37,783
But when we fired it up,

233
00:18:37,784 --> 00:18:40,537
a superpositional state was
briefly established inside.

234
00:18:44,041 --> 00:18:45,791
And you're not using quantum processors

235
00:18:45,792 --> 00:18:47,628
to manage the adaptive shielding?

236
00:18:54,051 --> 00:18:57,971
And I'm guessing your issue isn't
raw power so much as processing power?

237
00:19:10,859 --> 00:19:12,152
You're up early.

238
00:19:15,656 --> 00:19:16,657
Last night…

239
00:19:19,368 --> 00:19:21,787
I dreamt about the day
I asked your mother to marry me.

240
00:19:27,876 --> 00:19:30,212
I asked her if I could be your father.

241
00:19:30,921 --> 00:19:32,965
And I became her husband.

242
00:19:33,632 --> 00:19:38,345
I loved, honored and cherished her
for the rest of her life.

243
00:19:39,638 --> 00:19:41,265
When I became your father…

244
00:19:46,979 --> 00:19:48,772
I promised to protect you.

245
00:19:51,108 --> 00:19:53,485
No, Papa.

246
00:19:53,986 --> 00:19:56,196
You weren't even in the same world.

247
00:19:59,908 --> 00:20:01,952
You may not be my Daniela,

248
00:20:03,453 --> 00:20:07,583
but when I look at you, I see my daughter.

249
00:20:11,545 --> 00:20:13,088
I know what you mean.

250
00:20:22,139 --> 00:20:23,891
Oh, shit.

251
00:21:28,163 --> 00:21:29,164
Thanks.

252
00:21:35,796 --> 00:21:39,591
Hey. I was wondering…

253
00:21:40,509 --> 00:21:42,886
Well, you know,
it's Charlie's birthday today

254
00:21:44,263 --> 00:21:45,514
and I was gonna call him.

255
00:21:46,473 --> 00:21:47,766
Would you want to say hi?

256
00:21:49,685 --> 00:21:51,311
If you don't think it would upset him.

257
00:21:52,354 --> 00:21:53,939
Well, he's never known you.

258
00:21:56,191 --> 00:21:57,651
I think he'd like it.

259
00:21:58,569 --> 00:21:59,570
It wouldn't be weird?

260
00:22:01,113 --> 00:22:04,575
At this point, nothing is weird.

261
00:22:06,201 --> 00:22:07,202
Okay.

262
00:22:12,249 --> 00:22:13,332
Hello?

263
00:22:13,333 --> 00:22:16,752
- Hey, Eduardo, it's Charlie's mom.
- <i>Hey, Mrs. Stewart.</i>

264
00:22:16,753 --> 00:22:19,755
Hi, is he there?

265
00:22:19,756 --> 00:22:21,758
<i>No, he left yesterday.</i>

266
00:22:22,718 --> 00:22:23,719
Where'd he go?

267
00:22:24,553 --> 00:22:26,013
I don't want to get him in trouble.

268
00:22:26,597 --> 00:22:28,514
No, that's okay. You won't.

269
00:22:28,515 --> 00:22:30,892
I mean, he just said he was going
to be out of town for a few days

270
00:22:30,893 --> 00:22:32,102
to visit a friend.

271
00:22:33,687 --> 00:22:34,897
Which friend?

272
00:22:36,064 --> 00:22:37,566
<i>Someone named Max.</i>

273
00:22:42,613 --> 00:22:43,739
<i>Mrs. Stewart?</i>

274
00:22:45,490 --> 00:22:47,992
Yeah, thanks, Eduardo.

275
00:22:47,993 --> 00:22:49,828
Will you tell him that I called?

276
00:22:50,495 --> 00:22:52,623
<i>Yeah. Yeah, sure thing.</i>

277
00:22:57,419 --> 00:22:58,629
Everything okay?

278
00:23:16,063 --> 00:23:17,731
Did you just come through?

279
00:23:19,691 --> 00:23:20,734
Wait. Charlie?

280
00:23:25,447 --> 00:23:26,448
Charlie.

281
00:23:27,491 --> 00:23:28,909
How do you know my name?

282
00:23:29,451 --> 00:23:31,036
You're not in any danger.

283
00:23:31,620 --> 00:23:34,288
I'm… I'm Laurel.

284
00:23:34,289 --> 00:23:39,586
I'm sorry, I'm a little excited.
I've… I've never met a Charlie before.

285
00:23:41,213 --> 00:23:43,048
You've been traveling long?

286
00:23:45,175 --> 00:23:46,260
About a day.

287
00:23:48,470 --> 00:23:50,055
Any other injuries?

288
00:23:53,767 --> 00:23:55,060
What happened?

289
00:23:56,395 --> 00:23:57,980
Branch hit me.

290
00:24:00,315 --> 00:24:02,567
I was in a forest.

291
00:24:02,568 --> 00:24:03,651
It was dark.

292
00:24:03,652 --> 00:24:05,195
Something was chasing me.

293
00:24:05,779 --> 00:24:07,614
I'm glad you made it here.

294
00:24:18,667 --> 00:24:20,085
What is this place?

295
00:24:22,337 --> 00:24:23,589
My home.

296
00:24:24,631 --> 00:24:27,342
I was sleeping upstairs
a couple of years ago,

297
00:24:28,260 --> 00:24:30,636
and when I heard
what sounded like an explosion

298
00:24:30,637 --> 00:24:32,431
that shook the bones of the house,

299
00:24:33,891 --> 00:24:38,520
I ran downstairs to find three intruders.

300
00:24:39,563 --> 00:24:40,647
The box,

301
00:24:42,065 --> 00:24:45,986
a man named Jason Dessen,
and a woman named Amanda Lucas.

302
00:24:47,821 --> 00:24:48,905
You've…

303
00:24:48,906 --> 00:24:50,324
You've met my dad?

304
00:24:51,617 --> 00:24:52,701
A version.

305
00:24:58,874 --> 00:25:05,756
And you're just cool
with the box being in your house?

306
00:25:06,340 --> 00:25:09,092
Something like that.

307
00:25:10,928 --> 00:25:11,929
Are you hungry?

308
00:25:13,430 --> 00:25:14,473
Tired?

309
00:25:17,184 --> 00:25:20,020
Yeah, very tired.

310
00:25:21,146 --> 00:25:23,524
You're welcome
to sleep here for a few hours.

311
00:25:24,858 --> 00:25:28,737
I have a guest room upstairs,
but a lot of people stay…

312
00:25:29,404 --> 00:25:31,156
stay near the box.

313
00:25:32,950 --> 00:25:33,951
This…

314
00:25:34,743 --> 00:25:36,620
this couch pulls out.

315
00:26:13,782 --> 00:26:15,908
<i>Hey, you've reached the Stewarts.</i>

316
00:26:15,909 --> 00:26:18,495
<i>We can't come to the phone
right now, so leave a message.</i>

317
00:26:19,872 --> 00:26:22,207
<i>Jason, are you there?</i>

318
00:26:23,083 --> 00:26:24,668
<i>If you're there, pick up.</i>

319
00:26:25,711 --> 00:26:29,213
<i>I just called Charlie,
and Eduardo said he's been gone</i>

320
00:26:29,214 --> 00:26:31,550
<i>since yesterday, that he went to see Max.</i>

321
00:26:32,426 --> 00:26:34,510
<i>I'm at my dad's now,
but I'm gonna head back.</i>

322
00:26:34,511 --> 00:26:36,012
<i>I should be there in a few hours.</i>

323
00:26:36,013 --> 00:26:38,056
<i>I'm hoping maybe you've seen him.</i>

324
00:26:38,807 --> 00:26:41,143
<i>Okay, I love you.</i>

325
00:27:06,919 --> 00:27:09,253
Hey, call me
when you know what's going on.

326
00:27:09,254 --> 00:27:10,714
I will…

327
00:27:12,841 --> 00:27:14,885
- I'm still wearing your jacket.
- No, keep it.

328
00:27:19,932 --> 00:27:21,558
I don't know what to say.

329
00:27:23,519 --> 00:27:27,940
You know, the most important day of
your life is the day you meet yourself.

330
00:27:29,525 --> 00:27:31,067
And when you do,

331
00:27:31,068 --> 00:27:34,363
you're going to encounter
the same person I've always seen.

332
00:27:35,781 --> 00:27:41,245
The bravest, kindest,
most beautiful soul I've ever met.

333
00:27:43,705 --> 00:27:49,837
Knowing you, this version,
has been a perfect thing.

334
00:28:03,851 --> 00:28:06,478
If you ever need me, I'm here.

335
00:28:08,188 --> 00:28:09,731
Now, go find your son.

336
00:28:23,412 --> 00:28:25,163
Hey, I'll be right with you.

337
00:28:46,268 --> 00:28:47,393
Hey.

338
00:28:47,394 --> 00:28:48,395
Hi.

339
00:28:51,315 --> 00:28:57,236
Hey, you're the PI that I…
you know, helped me with the thing.

340
00:28:57,237 --> 00:28:59,948
- That's right. Mitchel MacNamara.
- Yeah.

341
00:29:01,617 --> 00:29:04,493
- Jason?
- Yeah, wow.

342
00:29:04,494 --> 00:29:06,205
- Good to see ya.
- You too.

343
00:29:07,247 --> 00:29:08,749
What can I get you?

344
00:29:10,167 --> 00:29:11,834
Your finest rye.

345
00:29:11,835 --> 00:29:13,169
- Rocks or…
- Neat.

346
00:29:13,170 --> 00:29:14,463
Neat.

347
00:29:18,634 --> 00:29:20,844
You and the family settling in okay?

348
00:29:21,929 --> 00:29:23,012
Yeah, yeah.

349
00:29:23,013 --> 00:29:29,060
Actually, we got a place
close by in Logan Square,

350
00:29:29,061 --> 00:29:32,355
and I work here and my son's in college.

351
00:29:32,356 --> 00:29:33,856
- Wow.
- Yeah.

352
00:29:33,857 --> 00:29:35,066
What's he studying?

353
00:29:35,067 --> 00:29:36,150
Architecture.

354
00:29:36,151 --> 00:29:37,569
- That's great.
- Yeah.

355
00:29:41,281 --> 00:29:43,200
Hey, it's quiet. I'll buy you a drink?

356
00:29:45,285 --> 00:29:49,872
Look, I'd… I'd like to,
but I got a lot of prep to finish

357
00:29:49,873 --> 00:29:52,876
before the happy hour rush, but thank you.

358
00:29:58,006 --> 00:30:00,425
Yeah, it's about to get
a little crazy in here.

359
00:30:05,889 --> 00:30:06,932
Another?

360
00:30:13,814 --> 00:30:14,982
What does it do?

361
00:30:17,401 --> 00:30:18,484
What does what do?

362
00:30:18,485 --> 00:30:19,736
Your cube.

363
00:30:21,488 --> 00:30:22,614
I'm sorry?

364
00:30:34,126 --> 00:30:35,169
Look.

365
00:30:37,546 --> 00:30:40,799
Look, if you want the information I have
to stay between us,

366
00:30:42,050 --> 00:30:44,218
I got to know what's going on.

367
00:30:44,219 --> 00:30:45,804
What information?

368
00:30:50,893 --> 00:30:55,022
These people in San Francisco,
they look very familiar.

369
00:31:00,944 --> 00:31:02,862
I'm gonna ask you
to leave right now. Let me see that.

370
00:31:02,863 --> 00:31:05,031
What does it do?

371
00:31:05,032 --> 00:31:07,325
Listen, you walk out
or I get someone to drag you--

372
00:31:07,326 --> 00:31:09,952
You and your family are
finally getting your footing here.

373
00:31:09,953 --> 00:31:12,622
Does your boss know you're not really
Jason Stewart?

374
00:31:12,623 --> 00:31:14,041
Does Charlie's school know?

375
00:31:14,958 --> 00:31:19,212
I'm not trying to mess up your life,
but maybe we can help each other out.

376
00:31:19,213 --> 00:31:20,796
All good, Jay?

377
00:31:20,797 --> 00:31:22,173
- Don't do this.
- Jason?

378
00:31:22,174 --> 00:31:24,050
Yeah, yeah, it's all good.

379
00:31:24,051 --> 00:31:25,052
I'll have another.

380
00:31:34,353 --> 00:31:35,561
All right.

381
00:31:35,562 --> 00:31:36,563
You know what?

382
00:31:37,648 --> 00:31:38,857
I will join you.

383
00:31:42,319 --> 00:31:43,654
Why not?

384
00:32:14,351 --> 00:32:16,102
I don't want any more trouble.

385
00:32:16,103 --> 00:32:17,187
Why would you?

386
00:32:18,522 --> 00:32:22,900
If I show you, you'll leave me
and my family alone forever?

387
00:32:22,901 --> 00:32:24,194
I give you my word.

388
00:32:24,736 --> 00:32:25,737
Yeah.

389
00:32:27,072 --> 00:32:28,073
Just give me a sec.

390
00:32:28,740 --> 00:32:30,449
- Hey, Matt.
- Yeah.

391
00:32:30,450 --> 00:32:31,660
- Can I--
- What?

392
00:32:32,494 --> 00:32:34,204
Listen, I hate to do this to you.

393
00:32:35,080 --> 00:32:36,163
What's going on?

394
00:32:36,164 --> 00:32:37,249
I need to take off.

395
00:32:37,916 --> 00:32:38,917
Everything okay?

396
00:32:40,085 --> 00:32:41,378
It's a family emergency.

397
00:32:43,297 --> 00:32:44,881
Go do what you gotta do.

398
00:32:44,882 --> 00:32:46,008
Thank you.

399
00:32:52,389 --> 00:32:53,473
All right.

400
00:33:00,272 --> 00:33:02,232
Are you sure that you want to do this?

401
00:33:03,984 --> 00:33:05,277
I have to do this.

402
00:33:15,454 --> 00:33:18,665
Well, <i>salud</i>.

403
00:33:28,133 --> 00:33:31,595
I will get my things.

404
00:33:39,061 --> 00:33:40,562
How did you pay for all this?

405
00:33:42,523 --> 00:33:47,652
So, it turns out my counterpart
in this world is a bit of a fuck-up.

406
00:33:47,653 --> 00:33:48,861
Yeah.

407
00:33:48,862 --> 00:33:50,905
Running Velocity into the ground.

408
00:33:50,906 --> 00:33:52,490
So, I took over.

409
00:33:52,491 --> 00:33:55,244
Brought some technological wonders
from other worlds.

410
00:33:55,911 --> 00:33:59,080
Now, Velocity is the premier tech company
that it should be.

411
00:33:59,081 --> 00:34:01,291
- Okay. Hello.
- I know. Thank you.

412
00:34:12,094 --> 00:34:14,887
Yeah, we're all from the same world.

413
00:34:14,888 --> 00:34:15,973
Blair.

414
00:34:19,226 --> 00:34:20,393
Hey.

415
00:34:21,228 --> 00:34:22,271
Hi.

416
00:34:22,896 --> 00:34:24,438
Wow.

417
00:34:25,274 --> 00:34:27,860
What are you doing here?

418
00:34:30,571 --> 00:34:34,949
I'm trying to help this guy
make better worlds.

419
00:34:39,996 --> 00:34:41,581
So, shall we eat?

420
00:34:42,875 --> 00:34:45,585
Yeah.

421
00:34:52,509 --> 00:34:55,387
Have you ever been inside?

422
00:34:58,390 --> 00:35:03,103
Heard enough stories to know that the
Corridor's no place for someone like me.

423
00:35:04,271 --> 00:35:08,483
But I am very curious.

424
00:35:12,070 --> 00:35:13,779
Can I get you anything else?

425
00:35:13,780 --> 00:35:14,906
No, thank you.

426
00:35:14,907 --> 00:35:18,451
But I should probably get going.

427
00:35:18,452 --> 00:35:20,579
No, no, no, no, you're my guest.

428
00:35:22,539 --> 00:35:23,916
Can I ask you why…

429
00:35:25,709 --> 00:35:27,252
you're in the box alone?

430
00:35:30,547 --> 00:35:32,841
You know what happened
to my father, right?

431
00:35:33,717 --> 00:35:37,595
He was kidnapped by a version of himself
and tried to take over his life.

432
00:35:37,596 --> 00:35:38,597
Yeah.

433
00:35:41,225 --> 00:35:44,061
In your world, did he ever make it back?

434
00:35:44,603 --> 00:35:45,646
He did.

435
00:35:48,190 --> 00:35:50,442
Along with a bunch
of other versions of him.

436
00:35:53,612 --> 00:35:54,655
Jason!

437
00:36:03,080 --> 00:36:04,081
Jay!

438
00:36:16,385 --> 00:36:17,718
Village Tap.

439
00:36:17,719 --> 00:36:19,220
Hi, it's Daniela.

440
00:36:19,221 --> 00:36:20,222
Is Jason there?

441
00:36:20,806 --> 00:36:22,974
No, he took off a couple of hours ago.

442
00:36:22,975 --> 00:36:25,102
He said he had a family emergency.

443
00:36:27,521 --> 00:36:30,231
Okay, if he comes back,

444
00:36:30,232 --> 00:36:33,359
will you tell him
that I called and I'm at the house?

445
00:36:33,360 --> 00:36:35,863
- <i>Sure thing. You got it.</i>
- Okay, thanks. Bye.

446
00:37:04,600 --> 00:37:06,018
Today's our birthday.

447
00:37:07,686 --> 00:37:08,937
Mine and Max's.

448
00:37:12,649 --> 00:37:13,775
Happy birthday.

449
00:37:14,818 --> 00:37:15,819
Thanks.

450
00:37:20,908 --> 00:37:22,492
A couple of days ago, my…

451
00:37:24,453 --> 00:37:31,001
my dad said he saw
a world where Max was still alive.

452
00:37:32,377 --> 00:37:33,962
And you want to meet him.

453
00:37:38,258 --> 00:37:40,844
This is your first time alone in the box.

454
00:37:41,803 --> 00:37:46,099
Everyone, including yourself,
has told me how scary a place it can be.

455
00:37:47,059 --> 00:37:50,646
And all that stuff swirling
around in your head isn't helping.

456
00:37:51,813 --> 00:37:52,981
What do I do?

457
00:37:59,780 --> 00:38:01,198
I have an idea.

458
00:38:03,075 --> 00:38:04,993
Would you excuse me for a moment?

459
00:38:13,293 --> 00:38:16,712
Seriously, it's kind of perfect, right?

460
00:38:16,713 --> 00:38:18,256
This world is…

461
00:38:18,257 --> 00:38:20,508
it's not spoiled, it's real.

462
00:38:20,509 --> 00:38:21,510
And what does that mean?

463
00:38:23,095 --> 00:38:25,513
Well, there aren't screens everywhere, right?

464
00:38:25,514 --> 00:38:28,516
People actually look at each other,
talk to each other.

465
00:38:28,517 --> 00:38:31,185
Well, you are bringing
a cell phone to market.

466
00:38:31,186 --> 00:38:32,645
She's got you there, pal.

467
00:38:32,646 --> 00:38:34,815
- What? No. No, no, no. That's not--
- Hey.

468
00:38:35,649 --> 00:38:37,693
Okay. Sorry.

469
00:38:38,527 --> 00:38:39,903
Excuse me.

470
00:38:50,747 --> 00:38:51,957
It's…

471
00:38:55,043 --> 00:38:56,712
It's really good to see you.

472
00:39:02,926 --> 00:39:04,928
What do you get up to in the box?

473
00:39:06,930 --> 00:39:09,349
You know, just try to survive.

474
00:39:10,184 --> 00:39:11,518
Just like you, I guess.

475
00:39:15,355 --> 00:39:16,690
I met him.

476
00:39:18,483 --> 00:39:21,904
The Jason whose life you stole.

477
00:39:23,780 --> 00:39:26,116
- Yeah.
- Yeah, he was with Amanda.

478
00:39:26,950 --> 00:39:30,329
The Amanda you up and left.

479
00:39:31,538 --> 00:39:32,539
Yes.

480
00:39:33,415 --> 00:39:37,669
They found me in a Velocity hangar,
and I was living in it.

481
00:39:39,296 --> 00:39:41,131
And they told me everything.

482
00:39:42,382 --> 00:39:44,842
You know, I'm not proud of the things--

483
00:39:44,843 --> 00:39:47,262
Well, that's a fucking relief, Jason,
because…

484
00:39:49,306 --> 00:39:51,141
it sounded pretty horrific.

485
00:39:52,017 --> 00:39:53,268
You know?

486
00:39:54,937 --> 00:39:56,438
Why are you here?

487
00:40:00,651 --> 00:40:02,361
Because I would…

488
00:40:02,945 --> 00:40:07,406
I would like to look at myself
in the mirror and not hate what I see.

489
00:40:07,407 --> 00:40:09,576
Then volunteer at a soup kitchen.

490
00:40:16,375 --> 00:40:18,377
Hey, I'm sorry about that.

491
00:40:20,295 --> 00:40:22,464
So, you…

492
00:40:23,090 --> 00:40:25,425
You tell him
about your read on the power issue?

493
00:40:26,218 --> 00:40:27,803
Yeah, she might have a fix.

494
00:40:32,724 --> 00:40:35,101
Okay, listen,
I wasn't gonna do this till later,

495
00:40:35,102 --> 00:40:36,228
but fuck it.

496
00:40:39,565 --> 00:40:40,857
We started strong, right?

497
00:40:40,858 --> 00:40:45,027
The middle, a little shaky,
but the end is about to be spectacular.

498
00:40:45,028 --> 00:40:47,405
What is happening?
What are you doing right now, Leighton?

499
00:40:47,406 --> 00:40:51,409
Blair, the three of us
built some amazing shit together,

500
00:40:51,410 --> 00:40:52,619
and here we are.

501
00:40:53,161 --> 00:40:55,997
We found each other again, right?

502
00:40:55,998 --> 00:40:57,123
It's fate.

503
00:40:57,124 --> 00:40:58,959
- Leighton.
- Come home.

504
00:41:00,169 --> 00:41:02,629
Come home and let me put
that brilliant mind of yours to work.

505
00:41:03,380 --> 00:41:04,798
We can finish what we started.

506
00:41:05,424 --> 00:41:06,425
Hey.

507
00:41:07,509 --> 00:41:08,594
Don't.

508
00:41:09,219 --> 00:41:10,304
What?

509
00:41:17,769 --> 00:41:20,022
So, at first, I was pretty lucky.

510
00:41:21,148 --> 00:41:22,566
Okay. I…

511
00:41:23,984 --> 00:41:29,156
went into the box
with so much positive energy.

512
00:42:01,063 --> 00:42:02,189
<i>And fear.</i>

513
00:42:03,857 --> 00:42:06,568
<i>I had fear too.</i>

514
00:42:37,349 --> 00:42:38,642
<i>And so, after that,</i>

515
00:42:39,226 --> 00:42:44,064
every world that I went to
was worse than the one before it.

516
00:42:45,691 --> 00:42:50,152
And so the more angry and scared I became,

517
00:42:50,153 --> 00:42:52,865
the more dangerous until…

518
00:42:54,783 --> 00:42:56,869
Oh, my God.

519
00:42:58,120 --> 00:42:59,538
{\an8}What the fuck?

520
00:43:02,124 --> 00:43:03,666
{\an8}Stop.

521
00:43:03,667 --> 00:43:05,294
{\an8}<i>I killed a whole world.</i>

522
00:43:06,420 --> 00:43:07,462
God.

523
00:43:10,507 --> 00:43:12,425
- No, no, you didn't.
- Billions of people.

524
00:43:12,426 --> 00:43:13,968
It wasn't you.

525
00:43:13,969 --> 00:43:15,219
And that's just the human toll.

526
00:43:15,220 --> 00:43:17,639
- You're not to blame for any of that.
- Yes, yes.

527
00:43:18,891 --> 00:43:20,392
Yes, I am.

528
00:43:21,101 --> 00:43:22,810
Because I built the box.

529
00:43:22,811 --> 00:43:24,229
I used the box.

530
00:43:26,523 --> 00:43:27,816
Bad things have happened.

531
00:43:28,400 --> 00:43:29,735
There's no argument there.

532
00:43:31,695 --> 00:43:33,447
And they may even happen again.

533
00:43:34,406 --> 00:43:38,784
But I have been to worlds that would have
gone dark if it wasn't for the box.

534
00:43:38,785 --> 00:43:40,579
Okay, we can still do some good.

535
00:43:42,080 --> 00:43:44,498
Okay, we'll…
We'll come up with guidelines, right?

536
00:43:44,499 --> 00:43:47,293
- Procedures, safety measures. Blair.
- The box is a disaster.

537
00:43:47,294 --> 00:43:48,711
I'm sorry, it is.

538
00:43:48,712 --> 00:43:51,047
- So, then you build a bigger one?
- Can you just…

539
00:43:51,048 --> 00:43:52,674
Are you out of your fucking mind?

540
00:43:53,717 --> 00:43:55,009
Blair.

541
00:43:55,010 --> 00:43:58,763
This is an uncontrollable,
unstoppable force.

542
00:43:58,764 --> 00:44:00,307
- Just please.
- Stop!

543
00:44:04,144 --> 00:44:05,437
Stop.

544
00:44:09,191 --> 00:44:12,945
Because if you think that you can manage
the unintended consequences of this shit…

545
00:44:16,406 --> 00:44:17,574
You can't.

546
00:44:40,848 --> 00:44:42,057
All right, lay it on me.

547
00:44:42,975 --> 00:44:47,271
How is there another family across
the country that looks exactly like yours?

548
00:44:48,021 --> 00:44:50,566
Why do people walk into this thing
and disappear?

549
00:44:52,025 --> 00:44:55,195
And how are you connected with Velocity?

550
00:44:56,864 --> 00:44:58,532
I'm really good at my job,

551
00:44:59,992 --> 00:45:03,452
but I can't even begin
to put the pieces of this puzzle together

552
00:45:03,453 --> 00:45:04,997
in a way that makes any sense.

553
00:45:06,748 --> 00:45:08,749
I've been all over this thing.
Inside, out.

554
00:45:08,750 --> 00:45:13,171
I can't find something
that even hints at what it does.

555
00:45:13,172 --> 00:45:14,173
Listen to me.

556
00:45:15,591 --> 00:45:18,593
I know this is all very confusing
and mysterious,

557
00:45:18,594 --> 00:45:22,097
but if I were you,
I would just walk away and not look back.

558
00:45:23,390 --> 00:45:26,185
Once you know, you can't unknow.

559
00:45:26,727 --> 00:45:30,397
Please, man, just trust me,
go fucking live your life.

560
00:45:31,148 --> 00:45:32,816
Forget about the box.

561
00:45:34,193 --> 00:45:35,527
Forget about me.

562
00:45:38,280 --> 00:45:40,115
Wow, I think you actually mean that.

563
00:45:40,824 --> 00:45:42,117
Like you don't even know.

564
00:45:44,828 --> 00:45:48,456
Look, I don't want to threaten you
or your family, but I need to know--

565
00:45:48,457 --> 00:45:49,458
Okay.

566
00:45:51,585 --> 00:45:52,794
You need to know.

567
00:45:59,134 --> 00:46:01,261
I think it's easier if I just show you.

568
00:46:12,981 --> 00:46:14,066
We going in?

569
00:46:14,691 --> 00:46:15,692
Come on.

570
00:46:19,071 --> 00:46:21,113
Now, right up the back…

571
00:46:21,114 --> 00:46:22,198
Here?

572
00:46:22,199 --> 00:46:24,076
There's a panel, down low.

573
00:46:26,870 --> 00:46:28,288
And you need to press it.

574
00:46:30,874 --> 00:46:32,459
Does it press in?

575
00:46:35,921 --> 00:46:37,047
Hey.

576
00:46:40,217 --> 00:46:41,343
I did warn you.

577
00:46:42,052 --> 00:46:43,387
Hey. Hey!

578
00:46:44,805 --> 00:46:45,806
Hey.

579
00:46:52,104 --> 00:46:53,272
Hey, knock it off!

580
00:46:59,069 --> 00:47:00,112
Hey!

581
00:48:24,780 --> 00:48:28,450
I've been struck
how interconnected everyone's stories are.

582
00:48:29,993 --> 00:48:36,500
One Jason theorizes that you're all being
pulled together by communal entanglement.

583
00:48:37,084 --> 00:48:40,337
Not everyone's adept
at navigating the box.

584
00:48:41,421 --> 00:48:46,218
But the ones who figure it out
all say the same thing.

585
00:48:46,760 --> 00:48:51,222
To get to where you wanna go,
you need to remember something.

586
00:48:51,223 --> 00:48:54,226
Ideally a person.

587
00:48:56,061 --> 00:48:59,857
I mean, I was four when Max died.

588
00:49:01,483 --> 00:49:03,360
Do you have any memories of him?

589
00:49:10,701 --> 00:49:14,955
I remember him in a hospital bed,
hooked up to a bunch of tubes.

590
00:49:22,087 --> 00:49:26,425
My mom has these videos
of us when we were younger…

591
00:49:29,261 --> 00:49:33,473
My favorite is this one of my dad.

592
00:49:34,641 --> 00:49:36,851
He's raking this huge pile of leaves

593
00:49:36,852 --> 00:49:40,479
and he just keeps pushing them
all around us.

594
00:49:40,480 --> 00:49:45,026
And the two of us
are just laughing and laughing

595
00:49:45,027 --> 00:49:47,321
as the pile gets bigger and bigger.

596
00:49:49,406 --> 00:49:55,621
I suspect that might
just take you where you wanna go.

597
00:50:14,139 --> 00:50:15,516
It's okay.

598
00:50:22,814 --> 00:50:24,233
Fuck this.

599
00:51:09,361 --> 00:51:11,070
What did they do now?

600
00:51:11,071 --> 00:51:12,990
They took bone marrow this time.

601
00:51:15,784 --> 00:51:18,829
Still looking for antigens
that they're not gonna fucking find.

602
00:51:19,872 --> 00:51:21,248
- Fuck.
- Are you okay?

603
00:51:22,916 --> 00:51:24,417
Thank you.

604
00:51:24,418 --> 00:51:25,751
Hey…

605
00:51:25,752 --> 00:51:27,253
Thank God.

606
00:51:27,254 --> 00:51:31,633
I'm Dr. Leighton Vance, and I've been
asked to speak with the both of you today

607
00:51:32,342 --> 00:51:34,010
because of my company's involvement

608
00:51:34,011 --> 00:51:36,013
with investigating
the Shikaakwa Park cube.

609
00:51:36,597 --> 00:51:38,598
Now, as you know, there was hope

610
00:51:38,599 --> 00:51:42,435
that since you both were able
to somehow rejuvenate

611
00:51:42,436 --> 00:51:45,438
from the Black Iris plague,
your bodies may contain the road map

612
00:51:45,439 --> 00:51:47,356
- to developing a vaccine.
- You know they--

613
00:51:47,357 --> 00:51:49,818
Unfortunately,
their testing came up empty,

614
00:51:51,195 --> 00:51:53,322
and all attention has been turned
to the cube.

615
00:51:54,364 --> 00:51:56,533
They're hoping
it's what somehow cured you.

616
00:51:57,409 --> 00:52:00,495
We already dismantled
the one that was moved to Millennium Park.

617
00:52:01,872 --> 00:52:03,331
Shit.

618
00:52:03,332 --> 00:52:06,667
The Black Iris plague is
spreading around the world,

619
00:52:06,668 --> 00:52:08,669
and the United Great Lakes has agreed
to cooperate

620
00:52:08,670 --> 00:52:11,423
with the international requests
to share all critical data.

621
00:52:11,924 --> 00:52:14,800
Which includes the cube and all
known information about the both of you.

622
00:52:14,801 --> 00:52:16,093
- What information?
- What the fuck?

623
00:52:16,094 --> 00:52:20,014
Representatives from the state union will
be here to collect you both in 30 minutes.

624
00:52:20,015 --> 00:52:23,143
Their methods of inquiry will become
considerably more intense.

625
00:52:25,437 --> 00:52:28,565
I brought you both a change of clothes.

626
00:52:33,487 --> 00:52:34,613
Leighton…

627
00:52:37,366 --> 00:52:38,742
Please help us.

628
00:52:40,994 --> 00:52:42,245
I'm sorry.

629
00:52:42,246 --> 00:52:45,874
I've done all I can do.

630
00:53:13,777 --> 00:53:14,778
Ryan.

631
00:53:46,643 --> 00:53:48,853
- Come on. This is Anthony.
- Hey.

632
00:53:48,854 --> 00:53:50,355
He'll take you the rest of the way.

633
00:53:51,815 --> 00:53:54,984
- Good luck.
- Wait, you're not coming with us?

634
00:53:54,985 --> 00:53:57,528
We hacked the cameras.
If I hurry, it'll be like I never left.

635
00:53:57,529 --> 00:53:59,363
- What if they catch you?
- No.

636
00:53:59,364 --> 00:54:00,865
I need to stay inside on this.

637
00:54:00,866 --> 00:54:03,118
- Why?
- For when you come back with the cure.

638
00:54:04,119 --> 00:54:05,661
This is my home.

639
00:54:05,662 --> 00:54:06,955
I have to make this right.

640
00:54:07,581 --> 00:54:09,541
I'm so sorry we brought this plague.

641
00:54:10,375 --> 00:54:11,501
You're good people.

642
00:54:11,502 --> 00:54:14,713
You just got caught up in something
that should have never been created.

643
00:54:15,589 --> 00:54:18,091
All right, you have eight minutes
to leave the building.

644
00:54:19,301 --> 00:54:21,052
Find the
doctors-without-multiverse-borders

645
00:54:21,053 --> 00:54:22,470
version of me.

646
00:54:22,471 --> 00:54:23,597
Bring back a cure.

647
00:54:24,348 --> 00:54:25,599
What's the plan?

648
00:54:26,350 --> 00:54:28,351
The closer we get to the box,
the more blockades.

649
00:54:28,352 --> 00:54:30,854
Hoping to get to Old Town
before we have to get too inventive.

650
00:54:40,572 --> 00:54:43,157
<i>Proceed to your assigned screening line.</i>

651
00:54:43,158 --> 00:54:45,786
- Move it! Move it!
- <i>This is for your own safety.</i>

652
00:54:50,332 --> 00:54:52,458
- Make your way to the end of the line.
- Hey, let go of me!

653
00:54:52,459 --> 00:54:55,211
<i>Leave all belongings, you only need
your helmet and identification card.</i>

654
00:54:55,212 --> 00:54:56,463
Next up. Let's go.

655
00:55:00,509 --> 00:55:02,427
{\an8}- It's positive.
- No. No!

656
00:55:39,256 --> 00:55:41,675
- How far are we from the box?
- Six blocks.

657
00:55:42,259 --> 00:55:46,220
From here, all street access
to the city's barricaded,

658
00:55:46,221 --> 00:55:47,763
but you can snake along the water.

659
00:55:47,764 --> 00:55:50,349
Your Lavender Wings
and your auto-injector are in there.

660
00:55:50,350 --> 00:55:51,642
I'm gonna give you guys some cover.

661
00:55:51,643 --> 00:55:54,188
All along there are naval mines.

662
00:55:54,855 --> 00:55:56,522
The plague decimated Michigan.

663
00:55:56,523 --> 00:55:58,065
It's to keep the refugees away.

664
00:55:58,066 --> 00:56:02,445
Oh, God, this place got really
authoritarian really fucking fast, huh?

665
00:56:02,446 --> 00:56:04,947
I'm gonna go out there,
I'm gonna trigger an explosion.

666
00:56:04,948 --> 00:56:06,867
- What?
- As safely as possible.

667
00:56:07,409 --> 00:56:09,744
It'll hopefully draw the attention
of those guarding the box

668
00:56:09,745 --> 00:56:11,121
and give you guys enough of an opening.

669
00:56:12,164 --> 00:56:13,956
- That's the best I can do.
- Okay.

670
00:56:13,957 --> 00:56:18,086
Hey, Leighton said
you guys would bring back a cure.

671
00:56:20,047 --> 00:56:21,131
My parents have it.

672
00:56:22,549 --> 00:56:23,550
We will.

673
00:56:24,384 --> 00:56:25,593
Thank you.

674
00:56:25,594 --> 00:56:27,387
Okay. Come on.

675
00:56:38,106 --> 00:56:39,483
- You okay?
- Yeah.

676
00:56:45,572 --> 00:56:46,823
Oh, fuck.

677
00:56:47,824 --> 00:56:49,159
What the hell was that?

678
00:56:50,869 --> 00:56:53,080
Shit. What do you think?

679
00:56:54,248 --> 00:56:56,667
We need to get the drone in the air!

680
00:56:57,251 --> 00:56:58,836
It kind of feels like now or never.

681
00:56:59,336 --> 00:57:00,420
Okay.

682
00:57:01,463 --> 00:57:02,506
Let's go.

683
00:57:03,173 --> 00:57:04,174
Come on.

684
00:57:16,103 --> 00:57:18,105
Hey! Stop!

685
00:57:21,441 --> 00:57:23,360
Stop or we will shoot!

686
00:57:26,363 --> 00:57:27,823
Oh, my God!

687
00:57:31,410 --> 00:57:32,411
Come on.

688
00:57:33,829 --> 00:57:35,664
- Come on, come on, come on.
- Shoot!

689
00:57:36,874 --> 00:57:38,124
Hey, hey, hey.

690
00:57:38,125 --> 00:57:39,667
Come here.

691
00:57:39,668 --> 00:57:41,503
Amanda, hey. Hey.

692
00:57:42,838 --> 00:57:43,922
Amanda!

693
00:57:45,215 --> 00:57:47,718
Amanda!

694
00:57:50,053 --> 00:57:51,680
Hey. Hey.

695
00:58:05,611 --> 00:58:06,820
Please, don't…

696
00:58:39,770 --> 00:58:40,771
<i>Max…</i>

697
00:58:44,316 --> 00:58:45,484
it's Charlie.

698
00:58:51,698 --> 00:58:52,950
I remember you.

699
00:58:58,997 --> 00:59:00,290
And he just…

700
00:59:01,250 --> 00:59:02,792
Let Blair walk out.

701
00:59:02,793 --> 00:59:04,044
Having seen everything?

702
00:59:05,671 --> 00:59:06,797
Everything.

703
00:59:11,552 --> 00:59:12,719
I want you to find her.

704
00:59:16,306 --> 00:59:18,225
And when you do,
she doesn't leave your sight.

705
00:59:19,977 --> 00:59:21,687
I wanna know everything she's doing.

706
00:59:46,295 --> 00:59:47,296
Oh, God.

707
00:59:56,513 --> 00:59:57,556
Fuck.

708
01:00:53,820 --> 01:00:55,239
Fuck! Yes!

709
01:01:04,915 --> 01:01:05,916
Yes.

710
01:02:15,903 --> 01:02:16,904
Hello again.

